<h2 id=3D"%F0%9F%93%9D-Knowledge-Check">=F0=9F=93=9D Knowledge Check<a clas=
s=3D"anchor-link" href=3D"https://github.com/ITAI1371-Group1/L04_Group1_ITA=
I1371/blob/2c56b9a34e775eee7482c4739c429f8d73537654//#%F0%9F%93%9D-Knowledg=
e-Check">=C2=B6</a></h2><p><strong>Instructions:</strong> Answer the follow=
ing questions in this markdown cell.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is the primary goal of Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)?</s=
trong></li>
</ol>
<p>The primary goal of Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is to thoroughly und=
erstand the structure, distributions, and characteristics of a dataset befo=
re applying any formal machine learning models or algorithms. It helps data=
 scientists discover hidden patterns, isolate extreme anomalies or outliers=
, detect missing values, verify underlying statistical assumptions, and fin=
d strong relationships between variables by combining descriptive statistic=
s with visual plots.</p>
<ol start=3D"2">
<li><strong>Based on the plots in this lab, what kind of person had the bes=
t chance of surviving the Titanic?</strong> (Describe them in terms of clas=
s, gender, and age).</li>
</ol>
<p>Based on the visualizations analyzed throughout this lab, the individual=
 with the highest statistical probability of survival would be a <strong>yo=
ung female child travelling in 1st Class</strong> who boarded the ship from=
 the port of <strong>Cherbourg (C)</strong> and paid a high ticket fare.</p=
>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gender:</strong> The gender plot showed an overwhelming histori=
cal survival bias toward females.</li>
<li><strong>Class &amp; Fare:</strong> The passenger class and fare plots c=
onfirmed that 1st Class passengers and those with expensive tickets had dra=
stically superior survival rates.</li>
<li><strong>Age:</strong> The age distribution histograms revealed a distin=
ct survival spike specifically for very young children, validating the "wom=
en and children first" protocol.</li>
</ul>
<ol start=3D"3">
<li><strong>Why is it important to visualize data instead of just looking a=
t summary statistics?</strong> What can a plot show you that a number like =
'mean' or 'count' can't?</li>
</ol>
<p>Visualizing data is crucial because basic summary statistics like a 'mea=
n' or 'count' compress thousands of complex data points into a single, loca=
lized metric, which can completely obscure the true reality of the dataset.=
</p>
<p>A plot can instantly reveal structural layout details that individual nu=
mbers completely miss, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Shape of Distributions:</strong> Plots show if data has mul=
tiple dense peaks (multimodal distributions) or if it heavily leans to one =
side (skewness).</li>
<li><strong>The Impact of Outliers:</strong> A boxplot visually isolates ex=
treme data points (like the $512 ticket fares) that mathematically distort =
a regular arithmetic mean.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden Structural Patterns:</strong> Distinct gaps, clusters, o=
r non-linear trends become immediately obvious to the human eye in a graphi=
c layout, whereas summary statistics treat all data points as a completely =
uniform group.</li>
</ul>

